<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tamale</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tamale is the capital of the Northern Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> and the largest city of northern Ghana. It became the administrative centre of the British Northern Territories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> in 1907, replacing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gambaga">Gambaga</a>, and grew as a market and route centre. Most of its people are Dagomba, and it is a centre of Islamic learning.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tamale lies in the Tamale Metropolitan District, which covers about 647 km2. The district borders the Sagnarigu District to the west and north, the Mion District to the east, the East Gonja District to the south and the Central Gonja District to the southwest. The city stands within the Kingdom of Dagbon, and is traditionally inhabited by the Dagombas. Gonja, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamprusi">Mamprusi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanumba">Nanumba</a>, Konkomba and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> people also live there.</p><p>The climate is tropical savanna (Köppen Aw), with a single rainy season each year. Mean monthly rainfall is about 89 mm, spread over some 95 days of showers, and mean monthly temperatures are around 34 °C, with minimums near 23 °C. Humidity is lowest between December and March, at about 30 percent, and highest around August, at about 70 percent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area of Tamale once consisted of small villages under the Kingdom of Dagbon, and it was a popular destination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> trails in the region. In 1907 the British decided to establish administrative headquarters there, with help from the Dakpema, after German officials had taken over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yendi">Yendi</a>. A road added in 1920, nicknamed the Great North Road, helped make Tamale an important centre of business in the Northern Territories.</p><p>Through the 20th century the population grew rapidly, as people looked for water and electricity, and land taken for infrastructure threatened residents' ability to farm. A series of disputes between the Gulkpe Na and the Dakpema over power in the town arose in part because British indirect rule created an imbalance between the chiefs. Local councils were later introduced and indirect rule was discontinued, but tension between the chiefs has continued. In 1960 Tamale became the capital of the Northern Region, after the region had separated from the rest of the north.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Tamale is the capital of the Northern Region and Ghana's third largest city, with 374,744 people at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Its economy rests on agriculture, trading, teaching and manufacturing. The first educational institution was a teacher training college of 1944, followed by a secondary school in 1951; British wishes to keep the Northern Territories agricultural and pastoral held education back, but the city has since become the educational hub of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>. It has the Tamale Teaching Hospital and Tamale International Airport, about 11 km from the centre.</p><p>Most residents are Muslim: 90.5 percent at the 2010 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Annual festivals include the Damba festival, Bugum chugu, Eid ul-Fitr and Eid Adha, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagomba-people">Dagbamba</a> perform dances such as the Baamaya and Tora. The Aliu Mahama Sports Stadium hosted some matches of the 2008 African Cup of Nations.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tamale, Ghana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shitobu's son Naa Nyagsi (r. 1416–1432) waged a war of expansion, overthrew many of the Tindaamba, set his sons, brothers and uncles over the conquered Konkomba, Nafeba, Basare and Chamba, and made his capital at Yani Dabari near <b>Tamale</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-dagbon">Kingdom of Dagbon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The boundaries were fixed by agreements of 1898-1899, and the protectorate was formally established in 1901, with its capital later at <b>Tamale</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-territories-of-the-gold-coast">Northern Territories of the Gold Coast</a></li></ul></section>
